#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096400 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29956ecb-a28a-5406-b8dc-9776e9a3ccee" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Polynomial Raised to a Power Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "19bf8d06-cbcc-547b-b882-ac89cd39fd1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-181" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = (3x^4+x^3)^5,$ calculate $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf = g^5, \\qquad g = 3x^4+x^3\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g}  = 5g^4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x} = 12x^3 + 3x^2.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =5{g}^{4}\\cdot (12{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =5(3{x}^{4}+{x}^{3})^{4}(12{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096401 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f0cdac9-57b2-5634-8d98-d6adf8a85204" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "8fcdb2c8-71ca-5bfa-b7fe-49f8f2c05bbb" :question/math-academy-id "e-182" :question/problem "Given that $y = \\sqrt{1-x^2},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}.\n$$\n\nHere, we've used $u$ instead of $g$ for the intermediate variable. \n\nLet $u=1-x^2.$ Then $y = \\sqrt u = u^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\dfrac{1}{2}u^{-1/2} = \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt u}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}  = -2x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-2x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{2\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096402 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a312840-0d35-50bb-94b3-b04778f4efc3" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Using the Chain Rule and Prime Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "2c37ae73-233d-5e0d-87ff-05cacc6d078b" :question/math-academy-id "e-183" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-3x}},$ calculate $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=1-3x$ and $f(g(x)) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{g}} = g^{-1/2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{-1/2})^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}{g}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2{g}^{3/2}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(1-3x)^{′}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{g}^{3/2}}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2{g}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2(1-3x)^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314997 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29956ecb-a28a-5406-b8dc-9776e9a3ccee" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Polynomial Raised to a Power Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db185f31-d898-509f-b9c4-a2881a9060fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28(1-7x)^3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db185f31-d898-509f-b9c4-a2881a9060fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28(1-7x)^3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313696} :answer-field/id #uuid "36ad8472-0eaf-547f-a51d-d9fd88b94c6d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45dae178-5aad-56cb-99e8-4cae2859e574" :question/math-academy-id "q-340690" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(1-7x)^{4}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{4},\\,g=1-7x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4{g}^{3},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-7.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =4{g}^{3}\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =-28(1-7x)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317321 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f0cdac9-57b2-5634-8d98-d6adf8a85204" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea0b074e-9124-53da-b940-4a0beab23775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316419} {:answer/id #uuid "4abe05c3-0f06-52ad-8909-cbe4491128f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2(5x+1)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4abe05c3-0f06-52ad-8909-cbe4491128f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2(5x+1)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524509} :answer-field/id #uuid "cae0bf8f-009f-5cd9-a958-5250470d215a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "65a56fb3-c803-55e2-851b-d52f4cc805e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-342764" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5x+1}}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=5x+1.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f0cdac9-57b2-5634-8d98-d6adf8a85204" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc8d190d-9c67-5acd-8583-f14a36220ea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc8d190d-9c67-5acd-8583-f14a36220ea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524684} :answer-field/id #uuid "f05471f2-c115-5266-86b4-2c4bebd70c45" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45936e93-e188-5489-9cc9-795dad15e0d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-342792" :question/problem "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+2x}$ at the point where $x=-1$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=3{x}^{2}+2x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6x+2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (6x+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{6x+2}{2\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-1} & ={(\\frac{6x+2}{2\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+2x}})|}_{x=-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-1)+2}{2\\sqrt{3(-1)^{2}+2(-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{2\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29956ecb-a28a-5406-b8dc-9776e9a3ccee" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Polynomial Raised to a Power Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e53a687-845e-5e38-b650-cd7d2e960b1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "192" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524465} {:answer/id #uuid "4c87e60d-6195-503b-b8b0-645b9615d373" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "384" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524511} {:answer/id #uuid "92b0203b-17bf-5bcc-b180-500fc847f62a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3,072" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524613} {:answer/id #uuid "ca95a77f-004b-588b-8c88-365d7dedd455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "768" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524712} {:answer/id #uuid "de1da834-e484-5065-82b1-ac2bfe5f309a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "758" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c87e60d-6195-503b-b8b0-645b9615d373" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "384" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524511} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a15449d-4ef5-5450-8663-fb7daf5af1c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a24feddb-3c6c-5b5d-be09-ecb6ef41d845" :question/math-academy-id "q-48622" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=(3{x}^{2}-10)^{4},$ find the slope at the point $(2,16).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nFirst, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{4},\\,g=3{x}^{2}-10\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4{g}^{3},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=6x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =4{g}^{3}\\cdot (6x) \\\\  & =24x(3{x}^{2}-10)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at the point where $x=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & ={24x{(3{x}^{2}-10)}^{3}|}_{x=2} \\\\  & =24(2){(3(2)^{2}-10)}^{3} \\\\  & =384.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097259 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a312840-0d35-50bb-94b3-b04778f4efc3" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Using the Chain Rule and Prime Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17e908ac-775f-57c5-b411-f2580198a03b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{(8x+6)}{\\sqrt{(2{x}^{2}+3x)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524427} {:answer/id #uuid "3e8e41b2-c271-53b2-95cd-a26907947e66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x+3}{\\sqrt{(2{x}^{2}+3x)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524499} {:answer/id #uuid "b770c35b-70a6-5c86-ad4d-dd5c5297261a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{(4x+3)}{\\sqrt{(2{x}^{2}+3x)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524674} {:answer/id #uuid "f1844554-3506-5984-a850-e337e7d94eec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{(4x+3)}{\\sqrt[3]{(2{x}^{2}+3x)^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524807} {:answer/id #uuid "f8192899-3a5d-5635-a01d-e3e96a9ce1d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{(4x+3)}{2\\sqrt{(2{x}^{2}+3x)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b770c35b-70a6-5c86-ad4d-dd5c5297261a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{(4x+3)}{\\sqrt{(2{x}^{2}+3x)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524674} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bd41183-7068-5455-b0e0-8cdceb8831b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2da730c2-fcbd-535d-85d1-2014785f31eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-48850" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+3x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=2{x}^{2}+3x$ and $f(g(x))=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{g}}=2{g}^{-1/2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =2({g}^{-1/2})^{′}=-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{g}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{{g}^{3/2}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(2{x}^{2}+3x)^{′}=4x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{g}^{3/2}}\\cdot (4x+3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4x+3}{{g}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4x+3}{\\sqrt{(2{x}^{2}+3x)^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a312840-0d35-50bb-94b3-b04778f4efc3" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Function Using the Chain Rule and Prime Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25c6804e-8060-5dff-bba1-acaf4df961ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32(8x-7)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524452} {:answer/id #uuid "a07a2ebe-3a0b-54c5-a1a1-e83b39b74dac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16(8x-7)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524636} {:answer/id #uuid "a9e6700f-f254-59c8-947f-7f6a658e28f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32(16x-7)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524655} {:answer/id #uuid "d56bf506-fd8c-5071-aec9-cd4f69c5a6e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16(16x-7)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524738} {:answer/id #uuid "ee2fea0e-928b-58ba-8851-272406927771" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8(8x-7)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25c6804e-8060-5dff-bba1-acaf4df961ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32(8x-7)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524452} :answer-field/id #uuid "37b56870-5595-52b0-ae23-7f96f4a87f21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "217bdf0c-2bc6-585f-ac52-fad4aec9092f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71954" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(8x-7)}^{4},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=8x-7$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{4}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{4})^{′}=4{g}^{3}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(8x-7)^{′}=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =4{g}^{3}\\cdot (8) \\\\  & =32(8x-7)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
